Ampère's law states that the line integral of the magnetic field \( \boldsymbol{B} \\) around a closed loop is proportional to the total current \( I \\) enclosed by the loop: \[ 

 \text{For a closed loop: } \oint \boldsymbol{B} \bullet d\boldsymbol{l} = \mu_0 I_{enc} 
 \\] where \( \mu_0 \\) is the permeability of free space.

The range of a probability value is defined as the set of possible outcomes for a probability measure, which is mathematically expressed as: 
\begin{equation} 0 \leq P(X) \leq 1 \end{equation} 
where \( P(X) \) is the probability of event \( X \). Therefore, the range is between 0 and 1 inclusive.
